WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.360
All right, this is a different sort of story.
Um, I don't want to go all like public service

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:06.360
announcement

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:12.450
on people, but I am so desperate to try to
figure out how we can solve this issue that

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:18.750
I feel obligated to say something about it
and that's that the texting while driving

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:25.110
just has to stop it. It is such a dangerous
behavior that is spreading like a virus. I

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:31.810
mean it's everywhere. And to be clear, I was
20 some years ago. I took more risks when

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:36.829
driving than I do today. I would go a little
too fast than I do a little faster than I.

00:00:36.829 --> 00:00:41.320
Then I go today when it's icy out. I would
look at text messages here and there while

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:46.969
driving, but then respond when I was stopped
at a traffic light. But the degree to which

00:00:46.969 --> 00:00:53.560
this behavior is leading to accidents and
deaths and destruction has become outrageous.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:57.990
I, I'm finding myself almost at a loss for
words because it is everywhere.

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:05.689
I saw it in London last week, someone looking
down and texting while driving and just rolling

00:01:05.689 --> 00:01:10.749
at 10 miles an hour, right through a red light
into an intersection and almost causing a

00:01:10.749 --> 00:01:17.999
crash. On the highway here in Massachusetts.
I 90 called the mass pike inside of Massachusetts.

00:01:17.999 --> 00:01:24.170
You can spot the texters from a mile away.
I start coming up on someone in the fast lane,

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:30.189
the left most lane and they're going 60 when
the speed limit is 65 and usually in the fast

00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:36.979
lane you're going 70 or 75 to get around people.
I go over to the middle lane, arguably dangerous

00:01:36.979 --> 00:01:41.399
in and of itself since you're not really supposed
to pass on the right but necessary to get

00:01:41.399 --> 00:01:47.979
away from these people and I look over and
the person has no idea that they are creating

00:01:47.979 --> 00:01:53.950
a backup in the fast lane looking down at
their phone and texting from a quarter mile

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:54.950
back.

00:01:54.950 --> 00:02:00.299
You see someone's sort of meandering left
and right, not staying in their lane. It looks

00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:05.590
like it could be a drunk driver, but it's
noon. Nope, not drunk. It's just someone on

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:11.320
their phone looking up only every other second.
And when you're going 40 or 50 or 60 you're

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:16.680
covering a lot of ground in that second when
you're not looking at the road here in Boston,

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:21.840
there are lots of sort of complicated four
and five way intersections. The city's known

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:27.280
for them. Some are always stopped, some are
two ways stops. Some are two. One ways that

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:34.120
meet and both have to turn onto another one
way and every week someone on their phone

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:41.879
misunderstands the intersection and almost
causes an accident that I see and I'm sure

00:02:41.879 --> 00:02:46.520
that lots of them become real accidents that
I don't see. The number of near misses that

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:51.840
I've had with people who are merely on their
phones is certainly in the dozens.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:58.190
And the thing about it is so many people who
do it think that their driving is not impaired.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:02.310
Distracted driving is shown to be like drunk
driving. And in a sense it could even be worse

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:08.019
because people who are slightly buzzed will
often because they know that they will pay

00:03:08.019 --> 00:03:13.640
extra attention when they're driving. Believe
me, I am not saying it's okay to drive when

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:19.489
buzzed. It's not. But people who do this with
their phones, they often don't say, Oh, I'm

00:03:19.489 --> 00:03:23.249
doing something that is dangerous so I should
pay extra attention. In fact, they're doing

00:03:23.249 --> 00:03:28.150
it the phone stuff because they believe that
it does not impair them in any way. And I've

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:31.720
actually had conversations with people who
say, you know, the thing about texting while

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:35.549
driving is some people can do it and some
people can't. Some people are good at it and

00:03:35.549 --> 00:03:36.650
some are not.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:41.689
I happen to be one of the people that can
do it. Uh, I'm not like those people who drive

00:03:41.689 --> 00:03:46.770
bizarrely when they are texting and I'm sure
that it's a bell curve to some degree. Different

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:50.810
people are affected to different degrees when
they're texting while driving, but with so

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:56.520
many people doing it, wouldn't you want to
have all of your attention on the road to

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:02.890
make sure that some other distract the driver
doesn't run a red light or stop sign and hit

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:11.200
you. This is an area where not only strict
but likely to be enforced. Penalties seem

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.909
to make a difference, but that's the key thing.
They have to be likely to be enforced because

00:04:15.909 --> 00:04:21.130
one of the things that we know is that it's
not just the severity of the punishment that

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:26.250
deters actions. We know that it doesn't in
many cases when you look at, for example,

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:31.460
the death penalty versus life in prison without
parole, it doesn't appear that the possibility

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:35.910
of the death penalty is a deterrent to people
committing murder.

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:39.970
But when you look at something like this or
when you look at, you know jaywalking is often

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:45.130
an example. It's a combination of not just
the severity of the punishment, but how likely

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:50.430
you are to get caught. I have friends in Sweden
who tell me, you know, I don't understand

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:56.940
what's going on in the U S the punishments
here are not only severe for drunk driving,

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:01.580
but they are likely to be enforced. We feel
like we are likely to be caught. So if we're

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:08.750
having one beer, maybe two, but in many cases,
even one beer, we don't drive. We get an Uber

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:13.770
or you get a designated driver or whatever,
you just don't chance it because it has taken

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:18.580
that seriously. I don't know what the solution
is here, especially with overloaded police

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:21.750
departments. Often by the way, overloaded
focusing on the wrong things like jailing

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:25.229
people for marijuana, but something has to
be done.

00:05:25.229 --> 00:05:29.750
I don't know what it is. And there it seems
to be beyond reason. I don't know if it's

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:37.039
related to decrease the tension spans where
simply driving alone is not enough stimulus

00:05:37.039 --> 00:05:40.500
for the brain where people feel like they
need to take out their phones and start scrolling

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:44.620
or texting. People are looking at Instagram.
I don't know what it is, but it's spreading

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:50.139
like a virus and I am open to hearing ideas
from people in the audience as to what it

00:05:50.139 --> 00:05:50.152
is that should be done.

